1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,514
It's the 80th minute.

2
00:00:01,514 --> 00:00:05,241
The ball goes over your head, and
your legs just won't go.

3
00:00:05,591 --> 00:00:08,664
In one classic study of pro players,

4
00:00:08,664 --> 00:00:15,724
high-intensity running in the last 15 minutes
was 35 to 45 percent lower than in the

5
00:00:15,724 --> 00:00:16,471
first 15.

6
00:00:16,821 --> 00:00:22,536
And substitutes who came on fresh ran 25
percent more at high intensity in that

7
00:00:22,536 --> 00:00:26,250
final spell than the players
who'd been on all game.

8
00:00:26,601 --> 00:00:28,152
So tiredness is normal.

9
00:00:28,152 --> 00:00:30,477
But you can hold it off for longer.

10
00:00:30,477 --> 00:00:31,188
Here's how.

11
00:00:31,787 --> 00:00:34,500
Football is mostly walking and jogging.

12
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,824
More than 70 percent of a match
is low intensity.

13
00:00:38,175 --> 00:00:45,646
But in top-level games, it's broken up by 150 to
250 short, intense actions: sprints,

14
00:00:45,646 --> 00:00:47,818
turns, jumps and tackles.

15
00:00:48,167 --> 00:00:49,835
So you need four things.

16
00:00:49,835 --> 00:00:52,704
A recharger, to refill you between bursts.

17
00:00:52,704 --> 00:00:56,107
A repeat-sprint battery, to
burst again and again.

18
00:00:56,107 --> 00:00:58,909
Smart running, so you don't waste energy.

19
00:00:58,909 --> 00:01:01,778
And a full fuel tank, so you
don't run dry.

20
00:01:02,128 --> 00:01:08,003
Even while they're mostly jogging, pros' hearts
average around 85 percent of maximum

21
00:01:08,003 --> 00:01:09,040
across a match.

22
00:01:09,390 --> 00:01:12,654
That's your aerobic system
working hard in the gaps,

23
00:01:12,654 --> 00:01:15,363
refilling the fuel your
sprints just burned.

24
00:01:15,713 --> 00:01:17,479
Think of a phone charger.

25
00:01:17,479 --> 00:01:19,720
Every sprint drains the battery.

26
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:21,758
The jog back is it on charge.

27
00:01:21,758 --> 00:01:25,697
A weak charger means you start the
next sprint half empty.

28
00:01:26,047 --> 00:01:29,281
In one famous study of elite
junior players,

29
00:01:29,281 --> 00:01:35,389
adding hard intervals twice a week for 8 weeks
raised their aerobic fitness by about

30
00:01:35,389 --> 00:01:36,180
11 percent.

31
00:01:36,531 --> 00:01:41,963
In matches, they covered 20 percent more
distance, made twice as many sprints,

32
00:01:41,963 --> 00:01:44,300
and got more touches of the ball.

33
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:49,869
It was a small study, but a review of 33
studies also found hard intervals boost

34
00:01:49,869 --> 00:01:51,038
players' fitness.

35
00:01:51,387 --> 00:01:55,345
Before any drill in this video,
warm up properly.

36
00:01:55,345 --> 00:02:01,520
A structured warm-up like FIFA 11+ cut
injuries by about 39 percent in trials.

37
00:02:01,871 --> 00:02:07,299
And if you ever get chest pain, feel faint or
get unusually breathless when you

38
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:11,641
exercise, stop, and see a doctor
before any more hard training.

39
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:15,034
The same goes if you have a
known heart condition.

40
00:02:15,383 --> 00:02:17,091
Drill 1: the 4 by 4.

41
00:02:17,091 --> 00:02:23,111
Run 4 minutes hard, so you can only say a
few words, then jog easy for 3.

42
00:02:23,111 --> 00:02:24,087
Do 4 rounds.

43
00:02:24,437 --> 00:02:25,311
New to this?

44
00:02:25,311 --> 00:02:28,540
A good starting point is 3
rounds, once a week.

45
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,154
Then build up gradually.

46
00:02:30,505 --> 00:02:32,813
Drill 2: small-sided games.

47
00:02:32,813 --> 00:02:39,655
A good starting point: 3 v 3 or 4 v 4, in
4-minute bouts with 2 to 3 minutes rest.

48
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:42,622
Fewer players makes the game harder.

49
00:02:42,972 --> 00:02:44,689
Drill 3: an easy base.

50
00:02:44,689 --> 00:02:50,887
Try 30 to 45 minutes of jogging, cycling or
swimming at a pace where you can still

51
00:02:50,887 --> 00:02:52,231
talk, once a week.

52
00:02:52,581 --> 00:02:53,560
The mistake?

53
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:55,670
Only doing long, slow runs.

54
00:02:55,670 --> 00:03:01,094
A stop-start running test tracked match
high-intensity running in pros.

55
00:03:01,094 --> 00:03:03,354
Plain aerobic capacity didn't.

56
00:03:03,704 --> 00:03:06,072
You need hard, stop-start work too.

57
00:03:06,772 --> 00:03:11,784
At the 2014 World Cup, players made
about 33 sprints a match,

58
00:03:11,784 --> 00:03:14,047
roughly one every 3 minutes.

59
00:03:14,397 --> 00:03:18,614
And here's what separated top pros
from lower-level pros.

60
00:03:18,614 --> 00:03:21,014
It wasn't mainly total distance.

61
00:03:21,014 --> 00:03:26,685
They did 28 percent more high-intensity
running and 58 percent more sprinting.

62
00:03:27,035 --> 00:03:28,807
Think of a camera flash.

63
00:03:28,807 --> 00:03:31,501
Part 1 decides how fast it recharges.

64
00:03:31,501 --> 00:03:36,038
This part makes the flash brighter,
and lets it fire more times.

65
00:03:36,387 --> 00:03:38,237
Drill 1: sprint quality.

66
00:03:38,237 --> 00:03:43,489
6 to 8 sprints of 20 to 30 metres, with a
slow walk back between each,

67
00:03:43,489 --> 00:03:46,153
early in the session on fresh legs.

68
00:03:46,153 --> 00:03:49,038
Experts recommend real sprint training.

69
00:03:49,388 --> 00:03:50,627
New to sprinting?

70
00:03:50,627 --> 00:03:56,066
For the first 2 weeks, do 4 to 6 at about 80
to 90 percent effort, then build.

71
00:03:56,066 --> 00:04:00,197
Skip max sprints if you have
hamstring, groin or calf pain,

72
00:04:00,197 --> 00:04:04,535
or you're coming back from a strain,
until a physio clears you.

73
00:04:04,885 --> 00:04:10,072
Drill 2: repeated-sprint sets, after a
few weeks of sprint quality.

74
00:04:10,072 --> 00:04:15,260
A good starting point: 1 to 2 sets of 6
sprints of 20 to 30 metres,

75
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:20,752
25 to 30 seconds rest between sprints, and
3 to 4 minutes between sets.

76
00:04:20,752 --> 00:04:21,973
Build to 3 sets.

77
00:04:22,323 --> 00:04:26,056
Stop the set if your times clearly
drop or your form breaks.

78
00:04:26,406 --> 00:04:27,712
Drill 3: 15-15s.

79
00:04:27,712 --> 00:04:32,857
15 seconds of fast striding, not
all-out, then 15 seconds walking.

80
00:04:32,857 --> 00:04:37,465
A good starting point is one block
of about 6 to 8 minutes.

81
00:04:37,465 --> 00:04:43,302
Over a few weeks, build to 2 blocks, with 3
minutes of easy walking between.

82
00:04:43,652 --> 00:04:49,660
In one study, pros added one block of fast
15-second runs, plus all-out sprints,

83
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:54,852
for 10 weeks, and their aerobic running
speed rose by about 8 percent.

84
00:04:55,202 --> 00:04:57,188
Now, protect your hamstrings.

85
00:04:57,188 --> 00:05:01,953
Hamstring strains are about 12 percent of
all injuries in pro football,

86
00:05:01,953 --> 00:05:07,447
and they increase toward the end of each half,
when players are likely to be tired.

87
00:05:07,797 --> 00:05:09,850
Nordic curls look promising.

88
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:15,584
In one trial of amateur players, they cut
hamstring injuries by about two thirds.

89
00:05:15,934 --> 00:05:20,337
But a later review called the
overall evidence inconclusive.

90
00:05:20,337 --> 00:05:22,142
So: promising, not magic.

91
00:05:22,842 --> 00:05:26,904
Lower yourself slowly and catch
yourself with your hands.

92
00:05:26,904 --> 00:05:31,245
Start with 2 sets of 5, once or twice
a week, after training,

93
00:05:31,245 --> 00:05:33,906
and not in the 2 days before a match.

94
00:05:33,906 --> 00:05:35,727
Expect sore legs at first.

95
00:05:36,077 --> 00:05:36,782
Under 18?

96
00:05:36,782 --> 00:05:40,237
Do sprint sets and Nordics with
a coach watching.

97
00:05:40,587 --> 00:05:42,514
And no sprinting through pain.

98
00:05:42,514 --> 00:05:46,553
If you feel a sudden sharp pain in the
back of your thigh, stop,

99
00:05:46,553 --> 00:05:48,480
and get it checked by a physio.

100
00:05:48,831 --> 00:05:49,709
The mistake?

101
00:05:49,709 --> 00:05:54,844
Turning sprints into a puke session, with
short rests and slower every rep.

102
00:05:54,844 --> 00:05:56,938
Sprint quality needs real rest.

103
00:05:57,287 --> 00:05:59,591
Football fatigue comes in two kinds.

104
00:05:59,941 --> 00:06:01,559
The first is temporary.

105
00:06:01,559 --> 00:06:05,200
In the 5 minutes after a player's
most intense spell,

106
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:09,650
high-intensity running fell about 12
percent below their average.

107
00:06:09,650 --> 00:06:10,864
Then it came back.

108
00:06:11,214 --> 00:06:12,668
The second builds up.

109
00:06:12,668 --> 00:06:15,246
That's the drop in the last 15 minutes.

110
00:06:15,596 --> 00:06:21,549
Elite players with very high first-half
workrates ran about 12 percent less at high

111
00:06:21,549 --> 00:06:23,463
intensity after the break.

112
00:06:23,463 --> 00:06:26,156
Moderate-workrate players didn't drop.

113
00:06:26,506 --> 00:06:28,646
Think of your phone's battery saver.

114
00:06:28,646 --> 00:06:31,711
It just stops apps you're not
using from draining it.

115
00:06:32,061 --> 00:06:34,265
Smart players don't stop working.

116
00:06:34,265 --> 00:06:35,432
They stop wasting.

117
00:06:36,132 --> 00:06:38,672
Habit 1: when the ball is far away,

118
00:06:38,672 --> 00:06:42,906
walk or jog into position instead of
drifting at half pace.

119
00:06:42,906 --> 00:06:45,799
Use throw-ins and goal kicks to breathe.

120
00:06:45,799 --> 00:06:48,057
That's when the recharger works.

121
00:06:48,407 --> 00:06:51,158
Habit 2: press on a trigger, as a team.

122
00:06:51,158 --> 00:06:52,946
A bad touch, a back-pass.

123
00:06:52,946 --> 00:06:54,871
Then everyone goes together.

124
00:06:55,221 --> 00:06:58,972
Habit 3: in the heat, expect less
high-speed running.

125
00:06:58,972 --> 00:07:03,209
World Cup players ran less at high
intensity in hot matches,

126
00:07:03,209 --> 00:07:07,168
but kept their total distance and
passed more accurately.

127
00:07:07,518 --> 00:07:10,975
No study has tested teaching
amateurs to pace.

128
00:07:10,975 --> 00:07:14,579
So treat these as smart habits,
not proven fixes.

129
00:07:14,929 --> 00:07:15,746
The mistake?

130
00:07:15,746 --> 00:07:19,012
Treating "never stop running" as
a badge of honour.

131
00:07:19,012 --> 00:07:21,713
All-action running early may
cost you late.

132
00:07:22,063 --> 00:07:24,431
The last part isn't training at all.

133
00:07:24,431 --> 00:07:25,071
It's fuel.

134
00:07:25,421 --> 00:07:29,115
Carbohydrate supplies most of
the energy in a match.

135
00:07:29,115 --> 00:07:35,041
After one match, about half of the thigh muscle
fibres were empty or nearly empty of

136
00:07:35,041 --> 00:07:36,365
their stored carbs.

137
00:07:36,715 --> 00:07:42,070
Players who started a game low on stored carbs
did much less high-speed running,

138
00:07:42,070 --> 00:07:44,054
especially in the second half.

139
00:07:44,404 --> 00:07:45,400
Think of a car.

140
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:48,884
A great engine won't help if you
start with half a tank.

141
00:07:49,234 --> 00:07:53,064
So, from UEFA's expert
guidelines for elite players.

142
00:07:53,064 --> 00:07:57,110
The day before, make carbs the
main part of every meal.

143
00:07:57,110 --> 00:08:00,867
Eat a carb-based meal 3 to 4
hours before kick-off,

144
00:08:00,867 --> 00:08:03,613
something familiar and easy to digest.

145
00:08:03,963 --> 00:08:08,802
After the warm-up and again at
half-time, take in some quick carbs,

146
00:08:08,802 --> 00:08:10,297
like a sports drink.

147
00:08:10,297 --> 00:08:13,286
The guideline is 30 to 60 grams each time.

148
00:08:13,635 --> 00:08:18,572
And drink enough that you don't lose more than 2
to 3 percent of your body weight in

149
00:08:18,572 --> 00:08:18,979
sweat.

150
00:08:18,979 --> 00:08:20,547
But don't overdrink either.

151
00:08:20,897 --> 00:08:24,758
In the heat, give your body 1 to 2
weeks of training to adapt.

152
00:08:25,109 --> 00:08:27,727
Feel dizzy, sick or crampy in the heat?

153
00:08:27,727 --> 00:08:31,130
Stop, get somewhere cool, cool
your skin and drink.

154
00:08:31,130 --> 00:08:33,813
You should feel better within 30 minutes.

155
00:08:34,163 --> 00:08:39,429
If you don't, or someone becomes confused,
collapses or has a fit, call an ambulance,

156
00:08:39,429 --> 00:08:41,694
and keep cooling them while you wait.

157
00:08:42,043 --> 00:08:42,843
The mistake?

158
00:08:42,843 --> 00:08:47,952
Playing an evening match on an empty stomach, or
a morning match with no breakfast.

159
00:08:48,303 --> 00:08:51,623
Here's a simple week, with a
Saturday match.

160
00:08:51,623 --> 00:08:52,656
Sunday: rest.

161
00:08:52,656 --> 00:08:54,352
Monday: easy base run.

162
00:08:54,352 --> 00:08:59,738
Tuesday: sprint quality, then 4 by 4s or
small-sided games, then Nordics.

163
00:09:00,087 --> 00:09:02,617
Wednesday: rest or easy work.

164
00:09:02,617 --> 00:09:05,991
Thursday: 15-15s or 1 to 2 sprint sets.

165
00:09:05,991 --> 00:09:09,111
Friday: rest, and carb-focused meals.

166
00:09:09,461 --> 00:09:14,304
Once the soreness has gone, add a second
Nordic session on Sunday or Monday.

167
00:09:14,654 --> 00:09:20,382
If your team trains midweek, that session takes
the place of Tuesday's or Thursday's

168
00:09:20,382 --> 00:09:22,202
work, whichever is closer.

169
00:09:22,202 --> 00:09:27,795
Keep it to 2 hard days a week, with an easy day
between, and give it 8 to 10 weeks.

170
00:09:28,145 --> 00:09:29,556
Build up gradually.

171
00:09:29,556 --> 00:09:32,732
Big jumps in training load
raise injury risk.

172
00:09:33,082 --> 00:09:34,511
Recharge in the gaps.

173
00:09:34,511 --> 00:09:36,526
Sprint, recover, sprint again.

174
00:09:36,526 --> 00:09:38,475
Spend energy when it matters.

175
00:09:38,475 --> 00:09:40,229
And start with a full tank.

176
00:09:40,579 --> 00:09:44,056
Do that, and in the 80th minute,
you're the one still going.
